# Infrastructure Economist Brief

**Run:** Quiet-by-Design Standards for MWAA Terminals
**Lens:** Airport capital-program economics and the marginal cost of specifying acoustic, PA, EVACS, FIDS, and tenant-technology performance before design lock.
**Audience:** MWAA board, capital planners, and policy readers who already know the run's thesis; this brief supplies the numerator, denominator, and comparator set the other agents will draw on.
**Date:** 2026-08-06.

---

## 1. Key findings

1. **The MWAA capital envelope has roughly tripled in eighteen months.** The 2025 U&L codified a $9.0B joint program ($6.99B IAD, $2.39B DCA) effective January 1, 2025 [Source: https://www.wusa9.com/article/news/local/virginia/metropolitan-washington-airports-authority-approves-9-billion-for-projects-at-dulles-reagan/65-f19e2e29-d5c4-4df8-a384-71ede2139a86]. The July 2026 White House–DOT–United–MWAA announcement raised the IAD share to $20–22.5B over roughly a decade [Source: https://www.aerotime.aero/articles/united-dulles-airport-unveil-20-billion-overhaul-including-new-concourses]. The design-standard question is therefore being asked at the exact moment when the largest airport program in MWAA's history is being scoped; if any specification is going to reach the drawings cheaply, this is the year to do it.

2. **A quiet-by-design specification is a rounding error inside that envelope, but only if it is specified early.** The MacLeamy curve — the mainstream owner's framing for design-stage vs. construction-stage decision cost — treats early-stage design changes as "keystrokes rather than change orders," with change cost rising exponentially through construction documents and into the field [Source: https://www.danieldavis.com/macleamy/]. Commercial acoustic-construction guides put post-construction sound-absorption retrofits at ~30–50% more than integrated design and structural sound-isolation retrofits at up to 2,000% of the original specified cost, before delay and revenue disruption [Source: https://commercial-acoustics.com/sound-advice/cost-of-acoustic-mitigation-construction-lifecycle/]. Applied against the ~$500M, 435,000 sq ft Concourse E baseline (~$1,150/sq ft) [Source: https://www.passengerterminaltoday.com/news/construction-architecture/washington-dulles-international-to-open-us500m-concourse-e-in-2026.html], even a $10–20/sq ft acoustic/PA/EVACS upgrade is <1.5% of the concourse hard cost when specified pre-award; retrofitting the same performance later exposes MWAA to seven- and eight-figure change orders per project.

3. **MWAA's debt-service headroom is narrowing at exactly the moment the capital program is expanding.** Moody's expects MWAA's net-revenue debt-service coverage to decline from 1.63x in 2024 to ~1.3x over the next five years, against a downgrade trigger at 1.10x [Source: https://www.bondbuyer.com/news/mwaa-coming-to-market-with-829-million-municipal-bond-deal]. MWAA plans to issue ~$5.5B of new debt between 2025 and 2028, pushing adjusted debt per O&D enplanement from $223 in 2024 to ~$400 by 2028 [Source: https://www.bondbuyer.com/news/mwaa-coming-to-market-with-829-million-municipal-bond-deal]. Any specification, however sensible, that requires a mid-program change order competes for the same thin coverage margin as the base program.

4. **The industry cost-overrun record makes "spec it later" the expensive option, not the safe one.** Berlin Brandenburg opened nine years late with a final cost of €6.5–7.3B against a €2.83B baseline (roughly a 2.3–2.6x overrun), driven substantially by late-stage fire-alarm and emergency-communications defects [Source: https://budgetoverrun.com/case-studies/berlin-brandenburg-airport]. LAX's Automated People Mover is now ~$880M over its $1.9B baseline and ~3 years late, with Fitch citing "construction delays, prolonged dispute resolution and difficulties in the parties' working relationship" as the drivers [Source: https://laist.com/news/transportation/lax-people-mover-delay-grand-jury-report]. Denver's automated baggage system, procured on a compressed schedule with the acceptance criteria loose, ran ~$560M over budget and 16 months late [Source: https://medium.com/@ketan.keshav7/the-400-million-lesson-what-the-denver-airport-baggage-fiasco-taught-us-about-escalation-and-c6f0bbc37305]. JFK New Terminal One's ~$9.5B build has slipped to at least November 2026, prompting Moody's to move the JFK NTO LLC outlook to negative at Baa3 [Source: https://simpleflying.com/6-billion-debt-clock-ticking-new-york-jfk-delayed-new-terminal-1/]. The counterexample, Heathrow T5, delivered £4.3B on time and on budget under the T5 Agreement's integrated delivery model [Source: https://www.mottmac.com/en-us/projects/heathrow-terminal-5/]. The T5 comparison matters: the projects that hit budget are the ones that specified interfaces (including acoustic and communications interfaces) before contracts hardened.

5. **MWAA already carries a local overrun of a different kind: designed-out scope at IAD.** The AeroTrain opened in January 2010 at ~$1.5B — but without a station at Concourse D, home to a majority of United's gates [Source: https://www.mwaa.com/business/d2-projects-aerotrain-system]. Sixteen years later, MWAA is now committing to extend AeroTrain and retire mobile lounges inside the $20–22.5B expansion. That is the airport-economics case study most directly relevant to the quiet-by-design question: a scope deletion at design stage produced a two-decade operational tax and a nine-figure follow-on cost. Under-specifying PA zoning, acoustic performance, or EVACS intelligibility in the coming C/D replacement is the same failure mode.

6. **The 2025 CPE tailwind is temporary.** IAD's 2025 signatory-airline CPE is $11.17, the lowest since 2005 [Source: https://www.ffxnow.com/2024/11/22/passengers-at-dulles-expected-to-grow-costs-to-airlines-drop-in-2025/]. That figure sits well below the industry mid-band for large hubs — third-party trackers place large-hub CPE at ~$8–18 with outliers above $20 [Source: https://dwuconsulting.com/dwu-ai/cost-per-enplaned-passenger]. As new debt lands on the rate base and $20–22.5B of IAD construction moves into service, CPE has only one direction to go. That is a further reason to lock quiet-by-design standards into the drawings now: airlines' price-sensitivity to change orders will be highest in the second half of the decade, when their base rate is rising anyway.

7. **The industry-wide capital-need signal is not ambiguous.** FAA's NPIAS 2025–2029 identifies ~$67.5B of AIP/BIL-eligible airport development need across 3,287 airports and ~18,100 projects — an 8% increase from the prior report [Source: https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/airports/planning_capacity/npias/current/ARP-NPIAS-2025-2029-Narrative.pdf]. ACI-NA's parallel 2025 study puts total five-year U.S. airport infrastructure need at $173.9B, up 15.1% in two years, against combined AIP/BIL/PFC funding of roughly $12B/yr versus needs above $30B/yr [Source: https://airportscouncil.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/2025-Infrastructure-Needs-Study.pdf]. The environment favors airports that can defend every dollar of scope on unit-cost and lifecycle grounds; a quiet-by-design standard is defensible on both if the numbers are done pre-award.

8. **The technical basis for a quiet-by-design specification exists in primary aviation research.** ACRP Research Report 175 (TRB, 2017) documents Speech Transmission Index (STI) as the objective measure of PA intelligibility and — for pier-style departure lounges — recommends a signal-to-noise ratio of ≥10 dB(A) and minimum STI of 0.45 for public-address broadcasts [Source: https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/24839/chapter/5]. NFPA 72 sets the higher STI ≥ 0.50 threshold for emergency voice/alarm systems in required areas. A design-manual standard grounded in these numbers is not novel or exotic; it is the codification of what airport engineers already treat as good practice. The economic question is not whether the standard is feasible, but whether MWAA specifies it into contracts before the drawings are complete or after.

