## 2. Evidence and analysis

### 2.1 Framing: this is a marginal-cost question, not a lifecycle-cost question

The temptation in airport-standards debates is to argue the lifecycle case: a quiet, intelligible, well-zoned terminal reduces missed boardings, complaint volumes, staff workload, and irregular-operations penalties, and those savings compound over the 30–50-year asset life. That argument is true and important, but it is not the argument that should carry a board vote. The board vote turns on marginal cost inside the *current* program envelope, because that is the money already committed.

The MWAA capital envelope moved from $9.0B in January 2025 [Source: https://www.wusa9.com/article/news/local/virginia/metropolitan-washington-airports-authority-approves-9-billion-for-projects-at-dulles-reagan/65-f19e2e29-d5c4-4df8-a384-71ede2139a86] to $20–22.5B in July 2026 [Source: https://www.aerotime.aero/articles/united-dulles-airport-unveil-20-billion-overhaul-including-new-concourses], with the Bond Buyer flagging the funding structure as open [Source: https://www.bondbuyer.com/news/dulles-expansion-raising-questions]. The IAD C/D replacement, an AeroTrain extension, and >5M sq ft of new or renovated space are inside that envelope. Concourse E is the near-term unit-cost benchmark at ~$500M / 435,000 sq ft (~$1,150/sq ft) [Source: https://www.passengerterminaltoday.com/news/construction-architecture/washington-dulles-international-to-open-us500m-concourse-e-in-2026.html]. Even a generous $20/sq ft acoustic, EVACS, PA-zoning, and FIDS-integration allowance is on the order of 1.7% of that unit cost — meaningful, but small next to the change-order risk of not specifying it. The relevant comparator is not "acoustic treatment vs. no acoustic treatment"; it is "$20/sq ft in the design manual" vs. "field change order after commissioning fails STI intelligibility testing."

### 2.2 The cost-of-change curve

The MacLeamy curve is the standard owner's tool for this framing: as a project progresses through predesign, schematic design, design development, construction documents, procurement, construction, and operation, the cost of implementing a change rises approximately exponentially while the ability to affect outcomes declines [Source: https://www.danieldavis.com/macleamy/]. Commercial acoustic construction guidance places acoustic-absorption retrofits at ~30–50% more than integrated design, and structural sound-isolation retrofits (mass, decoupling, penetrations) at multiples of original cost [Source: https://commercial-acoustics.com/sound-advice/cost-of-acoustic-mitigation-construction-lifecycle/]. That vendor-published figure should be treated with caution as a headline, but the underlying dynamic is not vendor advocacy — it is why every mature construction owner uses design-phase gates to freeze performance criteria. NFPA 72–grade EVACS intelligibility, in particular, is bound up with room finishes, ceiling geometry, HVAC background noise, and speaker layout; discovering a shortfall during commissioning almost always means either accepting the deficiency or reopening trades.

### 2.3 What the overrun record actually tells us

The industry's most-cited overruns are not stories of "too much scope." They are stories of scope specified badly or late.

- **Berlin Brandenburg (BER):** €2.83B baseline, €6.5–7.3B final, opened Oct 2020 (nine years late) [Source: https://budgetoverrun.com/case-studies/berlin-brandenburg-airport]. The May 2012 opening was cancelled 26 days out because fire-alarm and sprinkler power backup could not pass inspection [Source: https://www.airportwatch.org.uk/2020/10/nine-years-late-and-x3-over-budget-due-to-problems-berlins-brandenburg-airport-finally-opens-during-a-pandemic/]. Late-stage failure of the emergency-communications and life-safety systems drove roughly a decade of cost.
- **LAX APM:** $1.9B baseline, $880M overrun, delivery slipped from 2023 to mid-2026, with Fitch attributing the slippage to dispute resolution and party-relationship friction rather than raw construction difficulty [Source: https://laist.com/news/transportation/lax-people-mover-delay-grand-jury-report]. The Los Angeles City Council authorized an additional $400M to complete the project and settle claims [Source: https://cbsnews.com/losangeles/news/city-council-approves-additional-400-million-to-complete-laxs-automated-people-mover-settle-legal-claims].
- **Denver baggage:** ~$195M planned, ~$560M in overrun, 16 months late, and eventually abandoned by United in favor of a manual system while continuing to pay $60M/yr under the lease [Source: https://www.nbcnews.com/news/amp/wbna8135924]. The GAO's contemporaneous review of the delay documented direct financial impacts of the schedule slip [Source: https://www.gao.gov/assets/rced-95-35br.pdf].
- **JFK New Terminal One:** ~$9.5B, opening pushed from June to November 2026, Moody's outlook moved to negative at Baa3 [Source: https://simpleflying.com/6-billion-debt-clock-ticking-new-york-jfk-delayed-new-terminal-1/]. Financing exposure grows daily against a still-uncompleted asset.
- **IAD AeroTrain:** Opened January 2010 at ~$1.5B, but built without a Concourse D station [Source: https://www.mwaa.com/business/d2-projects-aerotrain-system]. That single scope decision has been reversed 16 years later, inside the current expansion.

Against those five, Heathrow T5 stands out: £4.3B, on time and on budget, delivered under the T5 Agreement, whose central innovation was owner-carried risk and integrated performance specification set before contracts were let [Source: https://www.mottmac.com/en-us/projects/heathrow-terminal-5/]. The transferable lesson for MWAA is not the delivery contract per se; it is that the projects that hit budget locked performance requirements — including acoustic and communications performance — before drawings hardened.

### 2.4 Debt, coverage, and the opportunity-cost argument

MWAA's revenue-bond ratings — Moody's Aa3, S&P AA-, Fitch AA- (all stable) [Source: https://www.mwaa.com/financial-statistics/investor-relations/credit-ratings] — are strong but not immune to the coverage compression that a doubled-or-tripled capital program will produce. Moody's specifically flags the DSCR trajectory (1.63x → ~1.3x) and the debt/O&D ($223 → ~$400) [Source: https://www.bondbuyer.com/news/mwaa-coming-to-market-with-829-million-municipal-bond-deal]. Every field-discovered change order that adds a few million dollars to a contract eats into that already-narrowing coverage margin, and does so at exactly the point in the amortization schedule when new-money issuance is heaviest.

The opportunity-cost frame is uncomfortable but honest: dollars spent retrofitting a PA system that failed intelligibility commissioning are dollars not spent on the AeroTrain extension, on Concourse C/D replacement, on the DCA Terminal 1 successor, or on debt-service coverage that keeps the AA-category rating. Getting the quiet-by-design specification into the design manual and into the technical sections of every construction contract issued after board adoption is the least-cost way to buy the outcome.

