## 3. Caveats and limitations

- **Vendor-published cost multipliers.** The "30–50% / up to 2,000%" retrofit-vs.-integrated-design figure comes from a commercial acoustics vendor's marketing content [Source: https://commercial-acoustics.com/sound-advice/cost-of-acoustic-mitigation-construction-lifecycle/]. The direction of the number is well-supported by MacLeamy and by mainstream construction-management literature, but the specific multiplier should not be quoted as a peer-reviewed finding. A stronger citation would be an ACRP synthesis or an owner-side change-order database; that was not retrieved inside this brief's time budget.
- **Concourse E unit cost is a proxy.** The ~$500M / 435,000 sq ft figure is drawn from trade press [Source: https://www.passengerterminaltoday.com/news/construction-architecture/washington-dulles-international-to-open-us500m-concourse-e-in-2026.html], not from a filed MWAA cost report. Using it as a MWAA-wide unit cost benchmark is an approximation. FFXnow reported the cost as "more than $700 million" in a later 2024 article; the delta reflects scope accretion during construction and reinforces this brief's point rather than undermining it.
- **MWAA CPE comparability.** The $11.17 (2025) and $12.88 (2024) figures are signatory-airline CPE per MWAA reporting [Source: https://www.ffxnow.com/2024/11/22/passengers-at-dulles-expected-to-grow-costs-to-airlines-drop-in-2025/]. Cross-airport CPE comparisons are notoriously non-standardized [Source: https://reason.org/commentary/airports-need-far-better-data-and-more-transparency/]; the "lowest since 2005" claim is MWAA's, not an independent audit.
- **Funding structure of the $20–22.5B expansion is publicly unresolved** as of this brief's date [Source: https://www.bondbuyer.com/news/dulles-expansion-raising-questions]. The DSCR and debt/enplanement figures cited assume the pre-expansion issuance plan; a substantially different funding mix (federal grant share, private participation, toll-road cross-subsidy) would materially change the coverage math.
- **Berlin BER is an imperfect comparator.** BER was a greenfield public-sector build in a very different regulatory and political environment; the transferable lesson is the failure mode (late-stage life-safety and communications noncompliance driving cost), not the numerical multiplier.
- **The airport-context packet flagged the current MWAA Design Manual and 2025 U&L MII thresholds as not verified in the context pass.** This brief has not independently retrieved those documents either; the "get it in the design manual" recommendation therefore assumes that MWAA's Design Manual is the right vehicle. If MII provisions require signatory-airline concurrence for standards that materially affect airline-leased premises, the delivery path is contractual rather than purely internal, and this brief has not costed that pathway.

